{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop","id":1482215,"title":"If the VTM mmorpg was released, would it have been a success or a flop?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-05-28T20:03:22-07:00","id":1482215,"title":"If the VTM mmorpg was released, would it have been a success or a flop?","content":"Most of you are vtm veterans, so you all probably know the reasons for the cancellation of the VTM mmorpg, it's for this reason I wont go into that. If you don't know about it, here's a link to a news site that talks all about it, ( <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2014/jun/05/world-of-darkness-the-inside-story-mmo-ccp-white-wolf\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.theguardian.com/technolo...ccp-white-wolf</a> ), you could also check out this other website that I got from the wiki, ( <a href=\"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/amazon-prime-6-free-games-for-june-2022-revealed/1100-6503873/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gamespot.com/articles/am.../1100-6503873/</a>).<br /> <br /> My question to you is if the game was released, would it have been a success or a flop?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/pub/18.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":27313,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","user":"Hello","date":". 05-28-2022, 11:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","name":"Hello","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27313_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-28T21:48:47-07:00","id":1482217,"title":"","content":"It came too late and would have failed. The golden age of the mmorpg was back in early WOW. The VTM mmorpg, like so many mmorpgs, took so long to develop that by the time it was nearly ready, the era of mmorpgs was already over.<br /> <br /> At best, it would be puttering along the same way DCUO does.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-28T22:22:06-07:00","id":1482218,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482217#post1482217\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It came too late and would have failed. The golden age of the mmorpg was back in early WOW. The VTM mmorpg, like so many mmorpgs, took so long to develop that by the time it was nearly ready, the era of mmorpgs was already over.<br /> <br /> At best, it would be puttering along the same way DCUO does.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I am legitimately surprised that DCUO is still alive, when so many other better mmos have died. Its probably because of the diehard DC fanbase that the DCUO hasn't died yet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/pub/18.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":27313,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","user":"Hello","date":". 05-29-2022, 01:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","name":"Hello","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27313_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-28T23:33:44-07:00","id":1482220,"title":"","content":"It would've likely flopped. Games, especially back in the day, had to release by a certain time finished or not. There's actually a logical reason behind this. The financial advisors and schedulers have to calculate how long developers can work on games without going over budget. It's also important for the advertisements to create maximum hype. Wait too long and interest dies out completely, and it becomes a net loss.<br /> <br /> That said, if the game had as many problems as the articles indicate, then it would have flopped when they were forced to release. That coupled with the waning popularity of MMOs, means it would fail some how based on current data.<br /> <br /> That said, MMOs are regaining popularity in recent years. However, recent games like V Rising have taken the spot of vampure mmorpg. Even if that wasn't a factor, if they began developing it again today, it wouldn't come out in time to maximize hype.<br /> <br /> TLDR, game would have failed regardless if it was released or not. Even if they tried again today, they can't make the game fast enough. Overall, the MMO is gone forever. For now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><div align=\"left\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/7.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26764-vilenecromancer","name":"Vilenecromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26764_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"247"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-28T23:47:07-07:00","id":1482222,"title":"","content":"I was really excited over it, but on reflection I think it would have underperformed if not flopped. It missed the golden age of MMORPG and of Vampire as well. As a game, it would have been neither fish nor fowl, it it would have to heavily diverge from the successful MMO formula to accommodate the vampire setting and I would have to diverge from the vampire setting to accommodate the online computer game. I feel that it would probably leave both MMO and V:tM enthusiasts unsatisfied. <br /> <br /> There would be some people who liked it, maybe enough to allow the game to last for a while, but it wouldn\u2019t be very successful in my opinion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-28T23:58:01-07:00","id":1482223,"title":"","content":"I think the question and responses suffer a lot from unstated assumptions.<br /> <br /> Define flop for example. Is an MMO that isn't financially profitable, but has a strong dedicated fanbase that are enough to justify keeping it up and running since there are other associated products with the brand that get increased profits from the increased buzz?<br /> <br /> It's not like CCP didn't understand the MMO market, the programming demands, or how to make a niche MMO work. EVE as never been a giant in the MMO world, but it's had longevity most MMO could never dream of.<br /> <br /> There's a huge amount of internal information we just don't have to really know if CCP was setting realistic goals for itself. There's a huge difference between making a movie for $10M that brings in $20M in the box office sales and is counting on some cult secondary market success to shore things up, and a film budgeted for $100M only doing $20M at the box office; there's just no way to recoup that kind of failure to adequately predict the market.<br /> <br /> Consider LotR Online, ESO, SW:TOR, and FF14 are all doing fairly well, are some of those aren't exactly spring chickens, knowing how to market to existing fans of an existing franchise was clearly not a bad idea of surviving the late 2000s early 2010s slump in MMOs.<br /> <br /> While I'm not so optimistic to claim it ever had a chance of being one of the top grossing MMOs ever, it reject the idea that there was some inherent level where a realistic level of success was impossible. Obviously CCP did the math and cut their losses, but that was after a considerable development cycle. Had that cycle gotten a functional game out instead of leading to a cancellation, I think it at least had a chance.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T01:09:10-07:00","id":1482226,"title":"","content":"The main issue with trying to convert a TTRPG to MMO is that pretty much every game group plays it a bit differently at their table. As such, however you design the MMO, it will not line up with large swaths of the player base.<br /> <br /> I remember some of the original development renders for the V:tM MMO and they were blowing huge amounts of development resources on things like how a long dress would trail properly going up or down stairs. I don't think a single person I have played with over the decades would have put the priority on clothing physics. This clothing focus continued into the V5 art work, and having recently watched reviews of Swansong, I have to say that V:tM is becoming more and more of a dress up dolly simulator during fashion week, than horror game.<br /> <br /> An MMO based in V:tM would end up being a dozen different games to cover everyone's needs. <br /> Bloodhunt for the combat junkies<br /> Swansong for the solo role players<br /> Bloodlines 1 for the people who are there for the story/replayability<br /> ESO for the people who want a lore rich world to ignore when they tea bag each other<br /> EVE for those who are into clan and faction related stuff<br /> Fortnight for the malkavians<br /> Dead by Daylight for the authentic sabbat vampire hunting mortals experience<br /> Warframe for the fashion frame end game<br /> <br /> There is no way to functionally develop an MMO with that diverse a player style and genre disparity.<br /> <br /> What's worse is that out of all the games I just listed, none of them would have worked for the types of games the various groups I have played with normally took part in.<br /> <br /> If the developers really wanted to burn resources for an online experience, then they should have sunk the development time into a WoD table top simulator equivalent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T03:43:55-07:00","id":1482236,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482226#post1482226\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This clothing focus continued into the V5 art work, and having recently watched reviews of Swansong, I have to say that V:tM is becoming more and more of a dress up dolly simulator during fashion week, than horror game.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's pretty accurate. \ud83d\ude02 I don't think it's anything new, though. The World of Darkness games have always had an element of style over substance. I don't mean that as a bad thing -- even in <i>Bloodhunt,</i> a fast-paced Battle Royale, half of the fun for me is dolling up my characters. I can certainly imagine how cosmetics would have been a major component of and source of revenue for the cancelled MMORPG.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1190-elphilm","name":"Elphilm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1190_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"763"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T04:13:59-07:00","id":1482238,"title":"","content":"Yeah cosmetics are one of the big generally tolerated microtransaction styles by video gamers. If you don't care, you don't spend money on them, but there's enough people that will drop a little for their favorite skins, and enough people that will drop hundreds on every last option, that a lot of games can go free-to-play and make their money mostly on cosmetics. So.... that's not inherently a wrong-headed priority.<br /> <br /> I also don't buy that the MMO needed to be everything to everyone to be successful.<br /> <br /> The HtR video games are pretty much antithetical to the HtR TTRPG. A lot of the TTRPG fans of HtR hated the video games on release because the promoted the worst shallow approach to the material prompted by the core books off-brand art. But... once you play them for what they are... they're solid games and the Hunter stuff adds a nice layer of depth and storytelling that most games of its genre don't have.<br /> <br /> Bloodlines 1 is immensely popular in the WoD fandom despite differences in playstyle. I've never play VtM in a fashion the resembles BL1... but I love playing the game (well more loved, I don't replay it as much as I used to).<br /> <br /> If the game is fun and good, people will play it. If its fun, good, and sells the preexisting brand properly, even more people will play it. People get that different media means different styles of approaching the brand. You don't need to create a video game where people feel like they're playing VtM how they're play VtM in a TTRPG. You need a video game that feels like it gets what VtM is supposed to be about broadly and does something neat with it regarding the play loop. There's a reason (well more than one really) why Redemption is a footnote and Bloodlines is a cult classic (though also a &quot;flop&quot; upon release); and it isn't because Bloodlines somehow managed to find this perfect balance of different TTRPG playstyles to appeal to. Bloodlines feels like a VtM vidego game. Redemption feels like a generic vampire videogame with VtM stuff tacked on. For example, in Bloodlines how you stat your character actually matters to how the story plays out,.as different traits can open up different dialogue options/etc. and make different paths easier or harder to complete. In Redemption how you stat out Christophe basically doesn't matter because the story plays out the same way regardless. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T09:23:01-07:00","id":1482256,"title":"","content":"The fantasy: a social intrigue game for roleplayers<br /> <br /> The reality: a violent gankbox for Call of Duty clans and PvP guilds looking for a new toy to break<br /> <br /> The project was doomed from the start. Even if it had made it to launch, it had no chance of pleasing the very different markets that would have been interested.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11599-reasor","name":"Reasor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T10:25:29-07:00","id":1482263,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482223#post1482223\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the question and responses suffer a lot from unstated assumptions.<br /> <br /> Define flop for example. Is an MMO that isn't financially profitable, but has a strong dedicated fanbase that are enough to justify keeping it up and running since there are other associated products with the brand that get increased profits from the increased buzz?<br /> <br /> It's not like CCP didn't understand the MMO market, the programming demands, or how to make a niche MMO work. EVE as never been a giant in the MMO world, but it's had longevity most MMO could never dream of.<br /> <br /> There's a huge amount of internal information we just don't have to really know if CCP was setting realistic goals for itself. There's a huge difference between making a movie for $10M that brings in $20M in the box office sales and is counting on some cult secondary market success to shore things up, and a film budgeted for $100M only doing $20M at the box office; there's just no way to recoup that kind of failure to adequately predict the market.<br /> <br /> Consider LotR Online, ESO, SW:TOR, and FF14 are all doing fairly well, are some of those aren't exactly spring chickens, knowing how to market to existing fans of an existing franchise was clearly not a bad idea of surviving the late 2000s early 2010s slump in MMOs.<br /> <br /> While I'm not so optimistic to claim it ever had a chance of being one of the top grossing MMOs ever, it reject the idea that there was some inherent level where a realistic level of success was impossible. Obviously CCP did the math and cut their losses, but that was after a considerable development cycle. Had that cycle gotten a functional game out instead of leading to a cancellation, I think it at least had a chance.</div> </div> </div> </div>A flop is when a game that doesn't provide a reasonable profit for the resources put into it. For single player games, developers usually see most of their profit within the first week or so. MMOs on the other hand, see profits last for long periods of time. However, they require additional resources to maintain. This means an MMO can flop years after release, even with a strong fan base if the developers or financial advisors make bad calls.<br /> <br /> The main factor would be the initial reaction to the game in this case, and if the pulled an ESO. If the game was good, but nobody really played it, it would flop. ESO almost flopped early on, but pulled through because developers started out listening to fans.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482226#post1482226\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The main issue with trying to convert a TTRPG to MMO is that pretty much every game group plays it a bit differently at their table. As such, however you design the MMO, it will not line up with large swaths of the player base.<br /> <br /> I remember some of the original development renders for the V:tM MMO and they were blowing huge amounts of development resources on things like how a long dress would trail properly going up or down stairs. I don't think a single person I have played with over the decades would have put the priority on clothing physics. This clothing focus continued into the V5 art work, and having recently watched reviews of Swansong, I have to say that V:tM is becoming more and more of a dress up dolly simulator during fashion week, than horror game.<br /> <br /> An MMO based in V:tM would end up being a dozen different games to cover everyone's needs. <br /> Bloodhunt for the combat junkies<br /> Swansong for the solo role players<br /> Bloodlines 1 for the people who are there for the story/replayability<br /> ESO for the people who want a lore rich world to ignore when they tea bag each other<br /> EVE for those who are into clan and faction related stuff<br /> Fortnight for the malkavians<br /> Dead by Daylight for the authentic sabbat vampire hunting mortals experience<br /> Warframe for the fashion frame end game<br /> <br /> There is no way to functionally develop an MMO with that diverse a player style and genre disparity.<br /> <br /> What's worse is that out of all the games I just listed, none of them would have worked for the types of games the various groups I have played with normally took part in.<br /> <br /> If the developers really wanted to burn resources for an online experience, then they should have sunk the development time into a WoD table top simulator equivalent.</div> </div> </div> </div>This just reminds me of a video I watched on game development. It said to summarize your game into a one sentence concept. All gameplay goes to reinforce that concept. It sounds like the developers weren't trying to do that, and were just taking vtm and putting it into a game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><div align=\"left\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/7.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26764-vilenecromancer","name":"Vilenecromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26764_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"247"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T11:44:03-07:00","id":1482274,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hello</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482218#post1482218\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am legitimately surprised that DCUO is still alive, when so many other better mmos have died. Its probably because of the diehard DC fanbase that the DCUO hasn't died yet.</div> </div> </div> </div>DCUO survives because it is a piece in a larger setup instead of the mainstay of its franchise. It never had to survive on its own.<br /> <br /> Curiously, A VtM MMO would have the same advantage, specially now with Paradox's approach.<br /> <br /> \u200bAnd as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> said, as long as it was good on its own as an MMO, it could still be a success for whatever it's worth, enough to remain played for many years and maybe even now.<br /> <br /> But talking on the matter of assumptions, I have to disagree with Heavy on the matter of what its development spelled.<br /> <br /> Unless you further state changes to its development, if the question is just if the game was released <i>ceteris paribus</i>, then it would almost certainly flop.<br /> <br /> The problems with bad development management were all over the place. The constant change in team and focus without ever getting anything really done was a huge issue. A game isn't just a good idea and programming skills, specially a big project. Mismanagement is the most effective killer for any big idea, as boring and unglamorous the basic management and logistics aspects are.<br /> <br /> While EVE made them seem as a powerhouse and good choice for this dev, actually CCP made itself into a one-trick-pony and was completely inadequate for it. To have a chance to ever see the light of day, ironically, the VtM MMO needed another developer entirely.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T12:25:06-07:00","id":1482279,"title":"","content":"The closest equivelant was the warhammer one which struggled as I recall and that had games workshop and ea's backing.<br /> I'd say probably not. Their was a lot of follow the leader in relation to wow back then. Unless the game was incredibly sincerly good and deviated from wows template it would hard to get a foot in.<br /> <br /> Frankly endemic issues within the gaming industry make me mildly surprised anything good gets made at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 05-29-2022, 03:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T13:23:33-07:00","id":1482284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482238#post1482238\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the game is fun and good, people will play it. If its fun, good, and sells the preexisting brand properly, even more people will play it. People get that different media means different styles of approaching the brand. You don't need to create a video game where people feel like they're playing VtM how they're play VtM in a TTRPG. You need a video game that feels like it gets what VtM is supposed to be about broadly and does something neat with it regarding the play loop. There's a reason (well more than one really) why Redemption is a footnote and Bloodlines is a cult classic (though also a &quot;flop&quot; upon release); and it isn't because Bloodlines somehow managed to find this perfect balance of different TTRPG playstyles to appeal to. Bloodlines feels like a VtM vidego game. Redemption feels like a generic vampire videogame with VtM stuff tacked on. For example, in Bloodlines how you stat your character actually matters to how the story plays out,.as different traits can open up different dialogue options/etc. and make different paths easier or harder to complete. In Redemption how you stat out Christophe basically doesn't matter because the story plays out the same way regardless.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think a more accurate way to look at it is Bloodlines was a V:tM game where everyone made their own characters and the ST lets things play out. Redemption on the other hand is essentially a premade one shot game. Both are very much V:tM but with different story structures. Both have multiple choice branches in the storyline, Redemption just saves that for the endings involving the love interest, rather than being part of most of the game play. By that metric, Redemption is more on par with something like Deus Ex: Human Revolution, a single PoV character, forcibly turned into something other than human, who goes through a non-branching storyline with minimal adjustments due to preferred combat style.<br /> <br /> To be fair though if you pick up the Ainkurn Sword, there is no reason to build Christoff for anything but melee. A blood draining katana with a trench coat for armor, so 90s.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T15:00:29-07:00","id":1482291,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vilenecromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482263#post1482263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A flop is when a game that doesn't provide a reasonable profit for the resources put into it.</div> </div> </div> </div>So... everyone's making a ton of assumptions because we don't know the resources put into it, or what the profit would have been if the development process had actually resulted in a releasable game (even if 'meh' one that could have been fixed over time like many MMOs have).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482274#post1482274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unless you further state changes to its development, if the question is just if the game was released <i>ceteris paribus</i>, then it would almost certainly flop.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not really far off from where I am. I just assumed that a scenario where &quot;if this game was released,&quot; would involve a lot of alterations to how things played out. The OP's hypothetical was if the game was released, not if a half-assed third attempt alpha slice was released. Having an actual game to release would have meant a lot of changes to what actually happened would have to be included.<br /> <br /> For example, addressing the management issues so the WoDMMO team could do it's job is a prerequisite change in this alternate history where a WoDMMO got released.<br /> <br /> I was also very focused on the previous posters making some pretty broad generalizations that the MMO was doomed based on timing/market alone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482284#post1482284\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Both are very much V:tM but with different story structures.</div> </div> </div> </div>What's really VtM about Redemption though? How many people consider &quot;playing a holy warrior cursed to be a vampire who then spends centuries asleep only to awake in the modern nights,&quot; a key part of VtM? Because the game spends a lot of time on that. How well does Redemption's &quot;Dark Ages&quot; section get the feel of the DA TTRPG material across vs. just being medieval fantasy at night? How well does the modern nights part do?<br /> <br /> Because that's one of the things BL1 gets a lot of praise for. The world in it feel gothic-punk. The music, the atmosphere, the NPCs, all feel like they were made specifically for a VtM sandbox. The Redemption ones are frequently criticized at how generic they are.<br /> <br /> The fact that Redemption is more of a set plot only makes it worse because the rail-road happy scenario doesn't use that format to its advantage to communicate VtM specific elements (the HtR video games don't have any plot choices at all, and do far more to communicate the feel of the WoD than Redemption does).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T15:59:49-07:00","id":1482298,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482291#post1482291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The OP's hypothetical was if the game was released, not if a half-assed third attempt alpha slice was released.</div> </div> </div> </div>In that case, we agree completely here.<br /> <br /> A lot of factors could make the game be a success. Heck, keeping a low goal to make small profit for small cost would have worked and even make room for further developments, like EA made a very good and successful series of Harry Potter games just by recycling the same engine over and over, with negligible updates to the core program.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T18:50:31-07:00","id":1482315,"title":"","content":"From what it sounded like, there were so many massive deep-rooted issues with the game that even if it was released, those issues would have kept dragging it down.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T20:20:21-07:00","id":1482330,"title":"","content":"Maybe. Look at any MMO (RPG or FPS or etc.) that's under Microsoft's umbrella: horrible development cycles, hated initial releases with years worth of fixing to do, but once the game is out Microsoft stops fucking with the game's dedicated teams and the games actually tend to improve significantly. This is because Microsoft has this really screwed up policy around how many long term employees their studios can have, and thus the development team are full of short term people that come in, do what they can, leave, and someone else gets hired and loses a huge amount of their time before being the next to go just figuring out what the short term before them was doing. Once the needs of the big starting development cycle is over, and a smaller more dedicated team of long term people can handle the vast majority of needs, that policy stops getting in the way and the games get better.<br /> <br /> I saw one video that made a strong case that Microsoft has basically tossed ~$50M worth of potential profits in a fire because of this policy killing the hype around initial releases, and forcing games to spend years getting to a point where people like them enough to start being profitable. Unlike CCP or even Paradox, it's entirely possible that Microsoft is such a big company that it doesn't actually give a shit because it looks like such a small blip in their quarterly reports, but from a perspective of the fans of those games that didn't have a say that Microsoft bought out the company that makes them, it's a gigantic pain in the ass to have to weight 4-7 years before the games actually are actually good and slowly start to bring in new players to be worth playing (since MMOs need people to be fun).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T20:46:57-07:00","id":1482333,"title":"","content":"Yeah, but CCP wasn't nearly big enough to even dream with the day they would be able to take those hits and just brush it off. Maybe they thought they could, but they couldn't. And they didn't had the resources to make changes to how the game is handled after release because they were already messing up royally with a dedicated team instead of just those temporaries.<br /> <br /> A lot of the problems with VtM were the spilling out of their problems with EVE, problems that they created themselves through bad management of resources. If they weren't screwing with their main product then sure, they could just delay VtM for a dozen more years and spend 10 more to make it good. But that was far from the case and they had to shut down the project entirely along with others. CCP (and EVE) barely survived and basically settled to stay a one-trick-pony.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-30T15:53:09-07:00","id":1482414,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482291#post1482291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's really VtM about Redemption though? How many people consider &quot;playing a holy warrior cursed to be a vampire who then spends centuries asleep only to awake in the modern nights,&quot; a key part of VtM? Because the game spends a lot of time on that. How well does Redemption's &quot;Dark Ages&quot; section get the feel of the DA TTRPG material across vs. just being medieval fantasy at night? How well does the modern nights part do?<br /> <br /> Because that's one of the things BL1 gets a lot of praise for. The world in it feel gothic-punk. The music, the atmosphere, the NPCs, all feel like they were made specifically for a VtM sandbox. The Redemption ones are frequently criticized at how generic they are.<br /> <br /> The fact that Redemption is more of a set plot only makes it worse because the rail-road happy scenario doesn't use that format to its advantage to communicate VtM specific elements (the HtR video games don't have any plot choices at all, and do far more to communicate the feel of the WoD than Redemption does).</div> </div> </div> </div>Wow, that's a lot to unpack there, where to start.<br /> <br /> The redemption of a fallen holy warrior who got turned into a vampire is exactly what V:tM wanted to model especially in its earlier iterations when the video game was made. Hence why we have faith mechanics, the Path of Humanity was the original default path, the concept one has to fight against ones vampiric instincts, etc. If anything the storyline more or less is a paint by numbers of everything in V:tM, except with premade characters rather than player made ones. I agree that the Bloodlines character creation and character sheet were more &quot;table top&quot; than Redemption, but the storylines and themes were about on par.<br /> <br /> Now you could say that it is some what old hat in vampire fiction, except that you would then have to contend with the fact that V:tM more or less copied most of its lore and concepts from other sources. To be fair many sources also copied V:tM so all of it at this point is one big pool of cliche fangs and blood.<br /> <br /> As for one game being more railroad and one being less is a personal taste thing, rather than saying anything objective regarding how much V:tM the product was. I have been part of games with both types of storytelling and as long as the story was good, it didn't matter if it was more on rails than other games.<br /> <br /> Now if you want to say that Redemption had many misteps by modern gaming standards, of course it does an old game. Most old games fail by our current standards. Hence why video game design is an evolving art form rather than a set in stone list of rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-30T16:54:50-07:00","id":1482422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482414#post1482414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Wow, that's a lot to unpack there, where to start.<br /> <br /> The redemption of a fallen holy warrior who got turned into a vampire is exactly what V:tM wanted to model especially in its earlier iterations when the video game was made. Hence why we have faith mechanics, the Path of Humanity was the original default path, the concept one has to fight against ones vampiric instincts, etc. If anything the storyline more or less is a paint by numbers of everything in V:tM, except with premade characters rather than player made ones. I agree that the Bloodlines character creation and character sheet were more &quot;table top&quot; than Redemption, but the storylines and themes were about on par.<br /> <br /> Now you could say that it is some what old hat in vampire fiction, except that you would then have to contend with the fact that V:tM more or less copied most of its lore and concepts from other sources. To be fair many sources also copied V:tM so all of it at this point is one big pool of cliche fangs and blood.<br /> <br /> As for one game being more railroad and one being less is a personal taste thing, rather than saying anything objective regarding how much V:tM the product was. I have been part of games with both types of storytelling and as long as the story was good, it didn't matter if it was more on rails than other games.<br /> <br /> Now if you want to say that Redemption had many misteps by modern gaming standards, of course it does an old game. Most old games fail by our current standards. Hence why video game design is an evolving art form rather than a set in stone list of rules.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I echo this, Redemption is one of the things that, if not brought me to Vampire, made me stay, so to speak. I started playing in Dark Ages Vampire. Dark Ages was what made me love the game and, consequently, the WoD when I was a kid (I've never been a great fan of games set on modern days, I just think the world is too &quot;boring&quot; and &quot;banal&quot; nowadays to give room for really dark and interesting themes, but that's just personal aesthetic preference).<br /> <br /> Also, the theme of redemption, as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar727_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"727\" data-vbnamecard=\"727\">Thoth</a> stated is at the very core of what vampire is supposed to be about. The fact that Redemption, as set in a medieval scenario is going to have similarities to other Medieval Fantasy doesn't at any rate diminish its values, on the contrary, as the familiarity to some aspects of something is what draws people to it, as when people dig deeper they're naturally going to discover the idiosyncrasies of the thing itself. We could always draw parallels in RPGs, it's always going to happen at the first glance, Redemption's second phase is set on the modern world and it plays really nice, I still laugh to this day of Christoff dialogues with Pink when he's freshly awakened.<br /> <br /> The game's soundtrack is wonderful, the dialogues are great and the actors who give voice to the characters are also great. The whole aesthetic is on another level compared to other games of the time and it there's also a lot of vtm lore marvelously put in it. The game suffered from some bugs that certainly hindered its success, it also had to compete with Diablo II, the story was very railroaded and it had perhaps too much combat in it, so it ended up not making a huge success, but it's still dear to my heart. <br /> <br /> One thing I'm going to say here, and I've been saying this for a long time, I'm 100% certain that if we had more games for WoD in general, the tabletop RPG would make A LOT more success. I still dream to this day of another Redemption-like game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-30T19:03:23-07:00","id":1482437,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482414#post1482414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The redemption of a fallen holy warrior who got turned into a vampire is exactly what V:tM wanted to model especially in its earlier iterations when the video game was made.</div> </div> </div> </div>What on Earth are you talking about?<br /> <br /> 1) Early VtM (or later VtM) has never been about holy warriors seeking redemption after being turned into a vampire. It's about undead monsters struggling to maintain some semblance of control as their supernatural urges and society of the damned push them further and further was from the light. Golconda is a myth, a legendary fable to encourage the idea that some form of redemption might be possible, but one only a tiny fraction of vampires dedicate themselves to finding, and only a tiny fraction of things get close.<br /> <br /> 2) Redemption came out in 2000 (only four years before Bloodlines, and it was developed during the same time as Revised, not the &quot;earlier iterations&quot; of the game). Don't talk like it came out in 1995 or something. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hence why we have faith mechanics, the Path of Humanity was the original default path, the concept one has to fight against ones vampiric instincts, etc. </div> </div> </div>The faith mechanics only matter before the embrace, and True Faith mechanics for mortal hunters have been part of VtM for ages. Humanity vs. paths isn't really part of Redemption even though it starts in the Dark Ages where the Roads would have been more prominent, and the humanity in Redemption is just picking the right dialogue options (that don't have any consequences attached to them like Bloodlines where you're picking between different rewards/penalties) and not killing random NPCs around you. Christof doesn't really struggle with his vampire urges at all unless you make him at the end, because his love is stronger from the beginning.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If anything the storyline more or less is a paint by numbers of everything in V:tM, except with premade characters rather than player made ones. </div> </div> </div>You're talking about a game where the Camarilla is completely absent in any meaningful way, and the plot is essential old school First Anarach Revolt members of the Sabbat vs. the more vicious Second Anarch Revolt members of the Sabbat? How is that &quot;paint by the numbers,&quot; VtM? At all?<br /> <br /> Chicago by Night is, by far, the exemplar of what the game intended as the most paint-by-the-numbers VtM experience. To the point where the original creators were somewhat unhappy with things meant to just be how Chicago was but free to be different in other locations got codified into The Way Things Are Done in default settings in general.<br /> <br /> Bloodlines plays off the Chicago template, Redemption has nothing to do with it. The problem isn't about flexible character creation, but that most of the core VtM Clans don't even matter to the story.<br /> <br /> Redemption is the game where you play a character that's essentially a Sabbat inquisitor that's fighting a Sabbat that's betrayed the Sect to their Antedeluvian instead of being an infernalist.<br /> <br /> How long has it been since you actually went back and played it? Or just read a decent plot summary here?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for one game being more railroad and one being less is a personal taste thing,... </div> </div> </div>What's the point of responding to something I said if you're just going to deflect from the question at hand?<br /> <br /> I didn't make any sort of argument about being more rail-roady of a plot was good or bad in general, but that Redemption's implementation of it was bad. You can literally get dialogue options to get characters to agree to things that they immediate ignore because that would disrupt the plot, and the main character and the NPCs all act like that never happened. &quot;You convince the NPC not to do X and to do Y instead, but since X is the plan, she does X even after promising to try Y, and this never comes up again,&quot; is not good rail-road plot writing. It's a lost opportunity to get into the duplicity of elders by weaving the betrayal into the plot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now if you want to say that Redemption had many misteps by modern gaming standards, of course it does an old game. </div> </div> </div>I'm comparing it to Bloodlines, that came out four years later, and the HtR games which came out two to three years after Redemption (2002 for the first, and 2003 for both sequels). I'm not comparing it to something from 2020. So this &quot;old game&quot; defense is painfully lackluster.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482422#post1482422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I started playing in Dark Ages Vampire. Dark Ages was what made me love the game and, consequently, the WoD when I was a kid</div> </div> </div> </div>As I've said countless times, Vampire: the Dark Ages was my first experience with the WoD and VtM. Redemption holds zero place in my heart despite the fact that I enjoy the DA era for VtM over the modern nights (not necessarily so for the other games). Redemption is Forever Knight with some VtM terms tossed around, not a solid VtM game.<br /> <br /> <br /> Even if it was just a game about the Omen War, it fails because it boils down to, &quot;Tremere are bad, but not quite as bad as Tzimisce, even so oppose both like a good rebel-against-The-Man Brujah,&quot; when you never really get to interact with organizations like the Fiefs of the Black Cross that are the DA precursors to the Camarilla. You get tossed in with the Anarchs and then Sabbat without ever knowing who they're rebelling against.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-30T20:48:41-07:00","id":1482452,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482437#post1482437\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Early VtM (or later VtM) has never been about holy warriors seeking redemption after being turned into a vampire. It's about undead monsters struggling to maintain some semblance of control as their supernatural urges and society of the damned push them further and further was from the light. Golconda is a myth, a legendary fable to encourage the idea that some form of redemption might be possible, but one only a tiny fraction of vampires dedicate themselves to finding, and only a tiny fraction of things get close.</div> </div> </div> </div>So having Goloconda and Children of Osiris from the very early editions and then adding Virtue Paths that were based on being redeemed or better individuals rather than being monstrous, is a bug rather than a feature in your mind?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482437#post1482437\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2) Redemption came out in 2000 (only four years before Bloodlines, and it was developed during the same time as Revised, not the &quot;earlier iterations&quot; of the game). Don't talk like it came out in 1995 or something.</div> </div> </div> </div>And video game development takes a couple years, back before studios could get away with &quot;early access&quot;, so yeah the initial storyboarding and themes used were probably worked up closer to 1998 than 2000.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482437#post1482437\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're talking about a game where the Camarilla is completely absent in any meaningful way, and the plot is essential old school First Anarach Revolt members of the Sabbat vs. the more vicious Second Anarch Revolt members of the Sabbat? How is that &quot;paint by the numbers,&quot; VtM? At all?</div> </div> </div> </div>Paint by numbers, good person gets forcibly turned into vampire, emos over the state of their soul, deals with repercussions of moral quandaries, beats up worse monsters, builds scooby gang of like minded good monsters, defeats bad guy, saves love interest to some degree. Hardly a revolutionary plot description for many &quot;table top grade&quot; V:tM games. <br /> <br /> In other words I am looking at the basics while you seem to be laser focused on representation of every aspect. I don't care if the factions are present or not, they used the setting and lore to tell a story that doesn't violate that setting &amp; lore, which is more than I can say for Bloodlines 2 if the leaks are to be believed.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482437#post1482437\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Chicago by Night is, by far, the exemplar of what the game intended as the most paint-by-the-numbers VtM experience. To the point where the original creators were somewhat unhappy with things meant to just be how Chicago was but free to be different in other locations got codified into The Way Things Are Done in default settings in general.<br /> <br /> Bloodlines plays off the Chicago template, Redemption has nothing to do with it. The problem isn't about flexible character creation, but that most of the core VtM Clans don't even matter to the story.<br /> <br /> Redemption is the game where you play a character that's essentially a Sabbat inquisitor that's fighting a Sabbat that's betrayed the Sect to their Antedeluvian instead of being an infernalist.<br /> <br /> How long has it been since you actually went back and played it? Or just read a decent plot summary here?</div> </div> </div> </div>A couple years ago, and only Wilhelm and Ecaterina are Sabbat in the modern age, what head canon are you adding to all this? Especially since Christoff is running Path of Humanity the entire time. Alternatively, are you playing this with mods or something?<br /> <br /> To be honest I have never liked any of the &quot;By Night&quot; books. They were poorly researched, badly implemented, and painfully boring.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-30T21:56:38-07:00","id":1482460,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482452#post1482452\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So having Goloconda and Children of Osiris from the very early editions and then adding Virtue Paths that were based on being redeemed or better individuals rather than being monstrous, is a bug rather than a feature in your mind?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think having the concept of Golconda is anything buy a feature and I posted exactly why.<br /> <br /> The Children of Osiris were an obscure group, not some essential element of the game, and the writers decided they were a bug and removed them from the game in Revised after they had more mentions in the two early Mummy books than they came up in vampire oriented supplements.<br /> <br /> The addition of &quot;virtue Paths&quot; is also something that game in later and wasn't a core part of the early editions of the game. They didn't really properly exist until 1996 with Roads in VDA. Before that the Sabbat and Black Hand Paths were all monstrous ones. Modern night Paths that were redemptive rather than just sticking to Humanity is a thing that started at the end of 2e and was mostly in Revise and V20.<br /> <br /> Do you even see how much you're shifting the goal posts around here too? It's very quickly starting to feel like facts aren't going to matter here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And video game development takes a couple years, back before studios could get away with &quot;early access&quot;, so yeah the initial storyboarding and themes used were probably worked up closer to 1998 than 2000. </div> </div> </div>So... you're agreeing with me? When do you think Revised VtM came out? When do you think developing it started to happen (since RPG core books take considerable time too)?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Paint by numbers, good person gets forcibly turned into vampire, emos over the state of their soul, deals with repercussions of moral quandaries, beats up worse monsters, builds scooby gang of like minded good monsters, defeats bad guy, saves love interest to some degree. Hardly a revolutionary plot description for many &quot;table top grade&quot; V:tM games. </div> </div> </div>But that's the exact problem. It's not &quot;paint by the numbers&quot; VtM, it's &quot;paint by the numbers&quot; generic 90s vampire fiction. The VtM specific stuff is tacked on to a generic vampire story, rather than a story the feels really made for the VtM experience.<br /> <br /> For example, especially in early VtM, &quot;good&quot; meant buying into the punk themes of the game, not &quot;good&quot; in a classic European hero of myth sort of way. Redemption hits the wrong notes by being Romantic instead of Gothic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In other words I am looking at the basics while you seem to be laser focused on representation of every aspect. </div> </div> </div>Seems to me more that you weren't expecting push back here and when I can cite specific things you're looking for any excuse to dodge them.<br /> <br /> Or to put it another way, if Redemption didn't have vampires that needed to feed off of blood to survive, and I complained that you need to that be a VtM game, you'd be saying I'm wrong for wanting &quot;everything&quot; represented. When I'm talking about really broad and really big aspects of the setting like the Camarilla and core book Clans not showing up, and you're painting me like I want every last bloodline to be present, it's hard to take you as seriously considering what I'm saying.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't care if the factions are present or not, they used the setting and lore to tell a story that doesn't violate that setting &amp; lore, which is more than I can say for Bloodlines 2 if the leaks are to be believed. </div> </div> </div>Oh, so now it's OK to compare thing to more recent games? Pick one. Last I checked we weren't supposed to be comparing to Redemption to games that came out in 2004 let alone ones that aren't even out yet.<br /> <br /> And I never said Redemption &quot;violated&quot; the lore and setting. I said it wasn't a good representation of what VtM feels like to play. I can think up all sorts of &quot;valid&quot; stories in VtM that are still completely off the wall compared to far more normal stories you'd see from most groups just playing with the core books and a few supplements, and wouldn't be anything like what most people would relate to their TT experiences.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A couple years ago, and only Wilhelm and Ecaterina are Sabbat in the modern age, what head canon are you adding to all this? </div> </div> </div>The modern coterie is: Christof, who's glad to be working with Wilhelm again, Wilhelm who's overtly Sabbat, Pink who's a mole, and Lily who's a Toreador who's sire uses humans for a sourceo of paint, and Lily doesn't have a problem with that despite being cautious about who she tells, which is literally an example of Toreador Antitribu style in the early books, and a Camarilla Nosferatu that doesn't seem to care much about working with Wilhelm despite his loyalty to the coterie being based on them saving him from Sabbat Nosferatu (wait, Redemption never violates the lore... but the Nosferatu are supposed to put Clan over Sect..). So, one full Sabbat, one mole, one main character that doesn't really have time to consider the Sects and joins with his Sabbat friend from before the Cam/Sabbat were founded,a character that's super heavily codes as an Antitribu even if she's not loyal to the Sabbat overtly, and a Cam vampire that ignores everything the Cam would actually require their members to do in this situation.<br /> <br /> And, I was talking about the main conflict of the game when I said what you quoted, which is that the main NPC factions are reflections of the First Anarch Revolt, and the Second Anarch Revolt, rather than the more standard Cam vs. Anarchs or Cam vs. Anarchs vs. Sabbat setups of most VtM games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To be honest I have never liked any of the &quot;By Night&quot; books. They were poorly researched, badly implemented, and painfully boring.<br /> </div> </div> </div>So? What does that have to do with the point?<br /> <br /> Or is this, &quot;Facts only count if I say they do.&quot;<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-31T04:06:06-07:00","id":1482470,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1482215-if-the-vtm-mmorpg-was-released-would-it-have-been-a-success-or-a-flop?p=1482452#post1482452\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And video game development takes a couple years, back before studios could get away with &quot;early access&quot;, so yeah the initial storyboarding and themes used were probably worked up closer to 1998 than 2000.</div> </div> </div> </div>Since I didn't played Redemption, I have no opinion on it, but this here isn't completely correct.<br /> <br /> While game development does take time, usually about 2 years, most of this time deals in the most basic programming aspects and graphic resources creation. The storyboard exists from a very early stage, but it gets to be updated and changed several times and can be so even considerably late in the game.<br /> <br /> Unless the game was devised without any attention to the setting, it should by all accounts consider material published as late as early 2000 for reference and mid 1999 for main story aspects that affect gameplay. That puts its timing well within early <i>Revised </i>and as far away as possible from early V:tM.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":".  correcting typo. 05-31-2022, 08:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}